call_details.response:
The board state has no immediate winning move for \(o\) (Player o) in any legal column. Likewise, \(x\) has no immediate winning placement in any currently accessible landing square. However, each side has exactly one cell that completes a four-in-a-row:

- For \(o\) the cell is \((0,1)\), completed by the already-present diagonal \(o\)s at \((1,2)\)-\((2,3)\)-\((3,4)\).  
- For \(x\) the cell is \((3,0)\), completed by the already-present horizontal \(x\)s at \((3,1)\)-\((3,2)\)-\((3,3)\).

Because of gravity these cells are not yet landings. The unique way for \(o\) to turn its cell into an immediate threat is to occupy the square beneath it, i.e. to play column 1. The unique way for \(x\) to turn its cell into an immediate threat is to occupy the square beneath it, i.e. to play column 0. In addition, after both of those threats have been answered, a further horizontal threat for \(x\) appears on row 0 whose key squares are precisely columns 2 and 4.

Consequently the game is a pure tempo battle for the right to use (and later to deny) those threats. The only first move that simultaneously

* refuses to surrender the first tempo,  
* claims the critical defensive square that permanently eliminates \(x\)’s secondary row-0 threat, and  
* leaves \(o\) a net tempo advantage after the forced exchanges  

is column 2. After the natural forced sequence

\[
o\text{ col }2,\;
x\text{ col }0,\;
o\text{ col }0,\;
o\text{ col }1,\;
x\text{ col }1,\;
o\text{ col }0,\;
x\text{ col }1
\]

the position is quiet, \(x\) possesses no further forcing reply, and it is again \(o\)’s turn to begin constructing a double threat on the right-hand side. Every other legal opening either hands \(x\) the net tempo or leaves the secondary threat alive, both of which are losing against optimal replies.

Final Answer: 2

